  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 306 PM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\n===== Thursday through Saturday =====\n\nBroad upper troughing will be over the central portion of the \ncountry for the end of the week and into the weekend. A sfc cold \nfront will drop south through the upper Midwest, though looks to \nstruggle to make it into our area by the end of the week as upper \nlevel support lacks the momentum to keep pushing the front south. \nHowever, we'll still see an increase in precip activity for this \ntime frame, with daily highs reaching the upper 80s to low 90s, and \nincreasing moisture to support isolated to scattered showers and \nstorms. For each day, expect afternoon diurnal convection to blossom \nas daytime heating results in 1500-2000+ J/kg of CAPE, however the \nsevere risk continues to appear low given the weak flow through the \nentire column and the lack of shear for any organized convection. \nAny storms on these days would be slow moving pop-ups, though PWATs \nup to 1.8-1.9\" will support some efficient rainfall rates. There \ncould be a low-end flooding risk if the same areas see multiple slow \nmoving or stationary storms. \n\n\n===== Sunday into Early Next Week =====\n\nLower confidence forecast going into next week as mentioned in the \nprior discussions, with our forecast leaning on the evolution of \nwestern US ridging and downstream troughing. If we end up with a \nmore expansive upper ridge across the US, a drier and warmer \nforecast is possible next week. However, an amplified type pattern \nwill be the contrast, with more precip chances into next week. We'll \ncarry on with the NBM's solution of a few rain chances next week but \nnot a wash out any particular day.",
